Browshot version 1.32.0
================================

Browshot (https://browshot.com/) is a web service to easily make screenshots of web pages in any screen size, as any device: iPhone, iPad, Android, PC, etc. Browshot has full Flash, JavaScript, CSS, & HTML5 support.

The latest API version is detailed at https://browshot.com/api/documentation. browshot follows the API documentation very closely: the function names are similar to the URLs used (screenshot/create becomes screenshot_create(), instance/list becomes instance_list(), etc.), the request arguments are exactly the same, etc.

The library version matches closely the API version it handles: browshot 1.0.0 is the first release for the API 1.0, browshot 1.1.1 is the second release for the API 1.1, etc.

browshot can handle most the API updates within the same major version, e.g. browshot 1.0.0 should be compatible with the API 1.1 or 1.2.

You can install the library from PyPI: https://pypi.python.org/pypi/Browshot

The source code can be found on github at https://github.com/juliensobrier/browshot-python



INSTALLATION

    python3 -m pip install .


REQUIREMENTS

    Python 3.9 or newer
    requests (installed automatically by pip)

TESTS

    python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -p '*_test.py'

LIVE TESTS

    Set BROWSHOT_API_KEY to enable read-only checks with your account.
    Set BROWSHOT_SCREENSHOT_ID to a finished capture and BROWSHOT_RETINA_ID to
    a finished capture with retina and ad blocking. For artifact checks, set
    BROWSHOT_PDF_ID, BROWSHOT_WEBM_ID, BROWSHOT_MP4_ID, and BROWSHOT_GIF_ID to
    owned, finished captures of each format.

API 1.32

    from browshot import BrowshotClient
    import os

    client = BrowshotClient(os.environ['BROWSHOT_API_KEY'])
    capture = client.screenshot_create('https://example.com/', {
        'instance_id': 373,
        'pdf': 1,
        'pdf_format': 'a4',
        'pdf_margin': '10mm',
        'device_scale': 2,
        'hide_ads': 1,
    })

Poll screenshot_info(capture['id']) until status is 'finished'. Download with
screenshot_pdf(capture['id']) or save with
screenshot_pdf_file(capture['id'], 'example.pdf').

PDF settings include pdf_format (letter, a4, or legal), pdf_background,
pdf_landscape, pdf_one_page, pdf_margin, and the per-side pdf_margin_top,
pdf_margin_bottom, pdf_margin_left, and pdf_margin_right.

Request a short video or animated GIF with the same capture method.

    recording = client.screenshot_create('https://example.com/', {
        'instance_id': 373,
        'video': 1,
        'video_format': 'gif',
        'video_duration': 5,
        'video_scale': 0.5,
    })

After the capture finishes, use screenshot_video(recording['id']) to get the
bytes or screenshot_video_file(recording['id'], 'example.gif') to save them.
Accepted formats are webm (default), mp4, and gif. Set video_duration to 1–30
seconds, video_quality to 0–63, video_fps to 1–30, and video_scale to 0.1–1.
GIF uses 20 fps. An MP4 request can produce WebM where MP4 encoding is unavailable.

The download methods return bytes or b'' on failure. File helpers use the
supplied path in an existing directory and return that path, or '' on failure.
Downloads preserve the returned bytes.

Choose a capture country through instance_id. The IDs below are in order
Chrome, Firefox, iPhone 12, and Samsung S20.

    France          373  374  375  376
    India           377  378  379  380
    Finland         381  382  383  384
    United Kingdom  385  386  387  388

Each new instance costs one credit before options. PDF adds one credit per
capture. Recording adds one credit per started five seconds of requested
duration. Both require a paid Chrome-based instance, and video requires
device_scale=1. Retina (device_scale=2), ad blocking (hide_ads=1), and downloads
add no credits.
